The '''2001 UEFA Super Cup''' was a [[Association football|football]] match between German team [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] and English team [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] on 24 August 2001 at [[Stade Louis II]], the annual [[UEFA Super Cup]] contested between the winners of the [[UEFA Champions League]] and [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]]. Bayern were appearing in the Super Cup for the third time, their two previous appearances in [[1975 European Super Cup|1975]] and [[1976 European Super Cup|1976]] had ended in defeat. Liverpool were appearing in their fourth Super Cup, they won the competition in [[1977 European Super Cup|1977]], and lost twice in [[1978 European Super Cup|1978]] and [[1984 European Super Cup|1984]].

The teams had qualified for the competition by winning the two seasonal European competitions. Both Bayern and Liverpool beat Spanish teams in the finals of the competitions. Bayern won the [[2000–01 UEFA Champions League]], defeating [[Valencia CF|Valencia]] 5–4 in a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] after the match had finished [[2001 UEFA Champions League Final|1–1]]. Liverpool won the [[2000–01 UEFA Cup]], beating [[Deportivo Alavés|Alavés]] [[2001 UEFA Cup Final|5–4]].

Watched by a crowd of 13,824, Liverpool took the lead in the first half when [[John Arne Riise]] scored. Liverpool extended their lead before half-time when [[Emile Heskey]] scored. Liverpool scored immediately after the start of the second half to lead the match 3–0 after [[Michael Owen]] scored. [[Hasan Salihamidžić]] and [[Carsten Jancker]] scored in the second half, but Liverpool held out until the end of the match to win 3–2, their second Super Cup win.

==Match==
===Background===
[[File:Stade Louis II.JPG|right|thumb|250px|The [[Stade Louis II]], which was the venue for the [[UEFA Super Cup]] from 1998 to 2012]]

Bayern Munich qualified for the Super Cup as the reigning [[UEFA Champions League]] winners. They had won the [[2000–01 UEFA Champions League]] beating [[Valencia CF|Valencia]] 5–4 in a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] after the match had finished [[2001 UEFA Champions League Final|1–1]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/champions_league/1346307.stm |title=Bayern crowned European champions |publisher=BBC Sport |date=23 May 2001 |access-date=21 August 2011 |archive-date=5 February 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060205140835/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/champions_league/1346307.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> It would be Bayern's third appearance in the Super Cup. Their two previous appearances in [[1975 European Super Cup|1975]] and [[1976 European Super Cup|1976]] against [[FC Dynamo Kyiv|Dynamo Kiev]] and [[R.S.C. Anderlecht|Anderlecht]] respectively had both ended in defeat.<ref name="super cup">{{cite web |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tabless/sup.html |title=European Super Cup |publisher=Rec. Sport. Soccer Statistics. Foundation |date=24 September 2010 |access-date=25 April 2011 |first=Karel |last=Stokkermans |archive-date=25 July 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120725234139/http://www.rsssf.com/tabless/sup.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Liverpool had qualified for the Super Cup as a result of winning the [[2000–01 UEFA Cup]]. They had beaten [[Deportivo Alavés|Alavés]] [[2001 UEFA Cup Final|5–4]] to win their third [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/may/16/minutebyminute.sport |title=Liverpool 5–4 Alaves |work=The Guardian |location=London |date=16 May 2001 |access-date=21 August 2011 |first=Matt |last=Biggs |archive-date=27 August 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180827174126/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/may/16/minutebyminute.sport |url-status=live }}</ref> Liverpool were appearing in their fourth Super Cup. They had previously won the competition in [[1977 European Super Cup|1977]] beating [[Hamburger SV]]. The two other appearances in [[1978 European Super Cup|1978]] and [[1984 European Super Cup|1984]] had resulted in losses to Anderlecht and [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] respectively.<ref name="super cup" />

Both sides had played several matches already, Bayern had already played four matches in the [[2001–02 Bundesliga]] and were fifth. They  had won two matches, drew one and lost one.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.dfb.de/bliga/bundes/archiv/2001/tabellen/e_bt1m04.html |title=Bundesliga Table Round 4 |publisher=Deutscher Fussball Bund (DFB) |date=28 August 2001 |access-date=21 June 2011 |archive-date=16 October 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121016183945/http://www.dfb.de/bliga/bundes/archiv/2001/tabellen/e_bt1m04.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Liverpool had played two legs in the third qualifying round of the [[2001–02 UEFA Champions League]]. They beat Finnish team [[FC Haka]] 9–1 on [[Playoff format#Total points series (aggregate)|aggregate]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/champions_league/1500956.stm |title=Liverpool stroll past FC Haka |publisher=BBC Sport |date=21 August 2001 |access-date=21 June 2011 |archive-date=2 October 2002 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20021002125842/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/champions_league/1500956.stm }}</ref> As winners of the [[2000–01 FA Cup]], Liverpool faced [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] in the [[2001 FA Charity Shield]], which they won 2–1.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/1487037.stm |title=Liverpool edge out United |publisher=BBC Sport |date=12 August 2001 |access-date=21 June 2001 |archive-date=20 April 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080420194953/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/1487037.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> Liverpool had also played one match in the [[2001–02 FA Premier League]]; a 2–1 win over [[West Ham United F.C.|West Ham United]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.lfchistory.net/SeasonArchive/Games/44 |title=Games for the 2001–02 season |publisher=LFC History |access-date=21 June 2011 |archive-date=1 October 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121001171823/http://www.lfchistory.net/SeasonArchive/Games/44 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Bayern and Liverpool had injury concerns ahead of the match. Four members of Bayern's Champions League winning side were missing through injury. [[Mehmet Scholl]], [[Stefan Effenberg]], [[Paulo Sérgio (footballer, born June 1969)|Paulo Sérgio]] and [[Jens Jeremies]] were all unavailable for selection for the German side. Liverpool were without [[Patrik Berger]] after he had undergone surgery on his knee.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/aug/24/newsstory.sport8?INTCMP=SRCH |title=Liverpool ready for Bayern in Super Cup |work=The Guardian |date=24 August 2001 |access-date=22 June 2011 |first=Dominic |last=Fifield |archive-date=4 December 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131204031430/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/aug/24/newsstory.sport8?INTCMP=SRCH |url-status=live }}</ref> Despite being injured for Liverpool's previous matches, [[Steven Gerrard]] was expected to feature.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/supercup/history/season=2001/round=1560/match=68022/report=rp.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120710083839/http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/supercup/history/season=2001/round=1560/match=68022/report=rp.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=10 July 2012 |title=Super Liverpool record Cup win |publisher=Union of European Football Associations (UEFA) |date=24 August 2001 |access-date=22 June 2001 }}</ref>

===Summary===
Bayern kicked off, but Liverpool had the first chance of the match. [[Michael Owen]] crossed the ball into the [[penalty area]] from the right side of the pitch towards [[Emile Heskey]], whose shot was deflected out for a corner.<ref name="guardian">{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/aug/25/newsstory.sport7?INTCMP=SRCH |title=Reds grab fifth pot in five-goal thriller |work=The Guardian |date=25 August 2001 |access-date=24 June 2011 |first=Dominic |last=Fifield |archive-date=10 November 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161110115958/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/aug/25/newsstory.sport7?INTCMP=SRCH |url-status=live }}</ref> Bayern responded immediately, although [[Owen Hargreaves]]' shot went high over the Liverpool goal.<ref name="minute">{{cite web|url=http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/supercup/history/season=2001/round=1560/match=68022/report=mbm.html |title=Minute-by-minute |publisher=Union of European Football Associations (UEFA) |access-date=24 June 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130105041244/http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/supercup/history/season=2001/round=1560/match=68022/report=mbm.html |archive-date=5 January 2013 }}</ref> Nine minutes after the start of the match, Liverpool were awarded a [[Direct free kick|free kick]] after a foul by [[Robert Kovač]] on Owen. The free-kick taken by [[Gary McAllister]] was met by [[Markus Babbel]], but he headed over the goal.<ref name="minute" /> With more fouls starting to occur, the first booking was awarded in the 14th minute. Liverpool midfielder [[Dietmar Hamann]] was shown a [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]] after he tackled [[Hasan Salihamidžić]] from behind.<ref name="minute" /> Bayern started to control the match midway through the first-half, however it was Liverpool who scored the first goal of the match. Liverpool defender [[John Arne Riise]] dispossessed Hargreaves, McAllister took control of the loose ball and passed to [[Steven Gerrard]]. Gerrard's pass to Owen meant he was in space down the right side of the pitch, Owen put a low-cross into the penalty area, which by-passed Heksey but found Riise who scored to give Liverpool the lead.<ref name="guardian" />

[[File:Michael Owen 072007 cropped.jpg|thumb|upright|left|alt=A man in a white sleeveless top, with another man in the background.|[[Michael Owen]] scored Liverpool's third goal.]]

Bayern were immediately on the attack following Liverpool's goal. A foul on Bayern defender [[Bixente Lizarazu]] by Gerrard resulted in a free-kick for Bayern. The subsequent free-kick taken by [[Ciriaco Sforza]] was headed over the Liverpool goal by [[Pablo Thiam]].<ref name="minute" /> Liverpool regrouped and their next attack almost resulted in another goal. Heskey's pass to Owen put him clear of the Bayern defence and one-on-one with [[Oliver Kahn]]. Owen attempted to chip the ball over Kahn, however the Bayern goalkeeper was able to save the shot.<ref name="minute" /> Immediately afterwards Bayern were on the attack. Bayern defender [[Willy Sagnol]] held off Riise and the crossed the ball into the penalty area, the cross was met by [[Giovane Élber]] whose header went wide of the Liverpool goal.<ref name="minute" /> With the first half coming to an end, Liverpool had another attack. Hamann passed the ball to Heskey on the edge of the Bayern penalty area, Heskey subsequently moved past Bayern defender's [[Thomas Linke]] and Kovač, and shot low beyond Kahn to give Liverpool a 2–0 lead.<ref name="guardian" />

Liverpool kicked off the second half, and within 13 seconds they had scored to lead the match 3–0. Liverpool defender [[Jamie Carragher]] kicked the ball into the Bayern half, defender Thiam missed his header, which meant the ball dropped to Owen, who controlled the ball with his right foot and then put the ball into the Bayern goal with his left foot.<ref name="guardian" /> Bayern reduced the deficit in the 57th minute when Salihamidžić scored. He headed the ball into the Liverpool goal from Bayern's first corner of the match to reduce the deficit to two goals.<ref name="minute" /> Midway through the half both teams made substitutions. Bayern replaced Sforza, [[Claudio Pizarro]] and Salihamidžić for [[Niko Kovač]], [[Carsten Jancker]] and [[Roque Santa Cruz]] respectively. Liverpool substituted Gerrard and Riise for [[Igor Bišćan]] and [[Danny Murphy (footballer, born 1977)|Danny Murphy]] respectively.<ref name="minute" /> Liverpool started to pass the ball around their midfield and defence in an attempt to waste time, however in the 82nd minute Bayern scored again. Substitute Jancker headed into the Liverpool goal from a cross by Elber.<ref name="guardian" /> Immediately afterwards Bayern had an opportunity to equalise, however Lizarazu's shot was straight at Liverpool goalkeeper [[Sander Westerveld]]. No further goals were scored and the referee blew for full-time with the final score 3–2 to Liverpool.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/international/owen-wraps-fifth-trophy-liverpool-9245354.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/international/owen-wraps-fifth-trophy-liverpool-9245354.html |archive-date=2022-05-01 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Owen wraps up fifth trophy for Liverpool |work=The Independent |date=25 August 2001 |access-date=24 June 2011 |first=Nick |last=Townsend }}{{cbignore}}</ref>

